> Olinger LK, Edmunds PJ, Levitan D, Smith TB, Lasker H, Feeley M, Mahoney L, Dahl A. Reproductive mode, but not rarity, influences population trajectories in corals. In Review 2026.

This page gathers the headline results of the study and pairs them with a tool to test the one choice they rest on. The figures below show the published analysis; open any program page for the full analysis and its updated version.

## Highlight results

### The integrated result


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Coral cover by ranked taxonomic abundance (common versus rare) and reproductive mode (brooders versus broadcasters). (A,C,E) Abundance distributions ranked by mean percent cover during baseline periods (2001–2005 for TCRMP; 2001–2005 for VINPS; 1992–1996 for CSUN). (B,D,F) Temporal trends of log₁₀-transformed percent cover. Taxa are grouped and colored by commonness (common: top 90% of cumulative cover; rare: bottom 10%) and reproductive mode. Trend lines represent model-derived slopes ± robust standard errors. (A,B) TCRMP species-level data; (C,D) VINPS species-level data; (E,F) CSUN genus-level data.](06_interactive_files/figure-html/fig-main-plot-1.png){#fig-main-plot width=960}
:::
