## Brooders and broadcasters over time

Reproductive mode reshapes the community. @fig-brooder-broadcaster-cover follows the mean cover of broadcasters and brooders separately in each program, and tracks the brooder share of total coral cover. Broadcaster cover falls across all three programs while brooder cover holds steadier, so the brooder share of cover climbs over the record. In two programs it approaches the 50 percent parity line, meaning brooders may soon hold as much reef cover as broadcasters, a reversal of the historical dominance of broadcasting reef-builders.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Brooder versus broadcaster cover over time, all taxa (as published). (A) Mean cover per site for broadcasters (solid) and brooders (dashed) in each program. (B) Brooder proportion of total coral cover, with the dashed line marking 50 percent parity.](06_interactive_files/figure-html/fig-brooder-broadcaster-cover-1.png){#fig-brooder-broadcaster-cover width=1152}
:::
:::



## Occupancy versus local cover

Two taxa can lose the same amount of cover in different ways: one by disappearing from sites (a loss of site occupancy), another by thinning where it persists (a loss of local cover). @fig-arrows-plot separates the two. Each arrow runs from a taxon's baseline site occupancy and local cover to its recent values. The taxa the manuscript names are colored by group and labeled; all other taxa are gray. Broadcasters tend to slide down and to the left (thinner and less widespread); several brooders hold or expand their occupancy.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Site occupancy (x) versus local cover (y), baseline to recent, per program (A TCRMP, B VINPS, C CSUN), as published. Site occupancy is the proportion of surveyed sites where the taxon was present; local cover is its mean percent cover at the sites and years where it was present. Labeled arrows, colored by group, are the taxa named in the manuscript; other taxa are shown in gray. Both axes are log scaled.](06_interactive_files/figure-html/fig-arrows-plot-1.png){#fig-arrows-plot width=1056}
:::
:::


